1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,000
We had a group camp and we got to this campsite and it was a really beautiful sort of lush campsite next to a river.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
We had a great weekend. I borrowed a tent. We put the tent up,

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Had a bit of difficulty hammering some of the pegs in because the ground was quite hard.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Instead of using the small rubber mallet, I borrowed a four pound mallet from one of our neighbours, hammered the tent pegs in without any hassles,

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Had a great weekend. There was a troop of monkeys in the forest across the stream and they were sort of coming to camp every now and then and we chased the monkeys away.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
We had a lot of fun exploring the area.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Time comes to pack up and I'm pulling tent pegs out of the ground and I pull one out and get hits in the face by a spray of water

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
And at this point I realised hammering it through a plastic water pipe which ran through the middle of the campsite.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
It's the fastest I've ever seen a tent move, because now this tent was being filled with water. so we put the tent up and ran away with it.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:18,000
But we were in quite a small town and in rural South African towns there isn't a plumber available on a Sunday afternoon to attend to these things.

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
So the campsite warden asked if I wouldn't mind leaving the tent peg there so that he could seal up the hole with it.

13
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:41,000
And I'm not sure what came after that, but I hope the end result was that they buried this water pipe a little deeper, because a plastic water pipe forages underground in the campsite seems like a race.

14
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You have to be giving credit for being able to find that water pipe.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And he was told that if there was a water dowsing badge or water divine badge, there wouldn't be the chin.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,000
It was a picture.

17
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Well, welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. this is Clark Green.

18
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:38,000
You're going to hear more from my friend Peter LaRue, who you heard just before the theme there a little bit later on, but we'll get to that in a moment.

19
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Heard from Frank Maynard this week.

20
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,000
He says nice interview with Hab Butler on the development of the Journey to Excellence program.

21
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
It's good to know they just didn't pull numbers out of thin air.

22
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:59,000
When I first heard about the Journey to Excellence I was skeptical that we were going to go back to the same arbitrary numerical system that the quality unit used before the four centennial quality unit years,

23
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
But after seeing the worksheets I realized a lot of thought went into it.

24
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Hab summed up what the program is all about.

25
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Units that do scouting the way scouting is done will have no difficulty earning high marks in the Journey to Excellence.

26
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Any shortcomings in one or more of the categories are leading indicators of what needs improvement in the unit,

27
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:27,000
And right now is a good time to run your own unit's numbers so far this year and see how you're doing, while there's time to work on anything that needs attention.

28
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Well thanks, Frank. that's a good comment

29
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:38,000
And it's indicative of the different feedback I've gotten about the Journey to Excellence program and kind of reflects my own feelings about it too.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Very positive thing.

31
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:53,000
If you didn't listen to last week's podcast, we were talking with Hab Butler, who is a regional vice president for the BSA and was the chair of the Journey to Excellence development working group.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,000
He had some things to say about the way that things were developed and what the hopes are for that program.

33
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
I also heard this week from Tony Rotol.

34
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Tony is from Troop 457 in Cordova, Tennessee,

35
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
And he wrote in to tell me a little bit about his dad,

36
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And his dad is up there in St Charles Illinois,

37
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
A little farther north,

38
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
A lot farther north,

39
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:25,000
And his dad's, with Troop 13, was with Troop 969 in Chicago for about 50 years.

40
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Tony is rightfully proud of his pop there and said that he finally convinced him to start listening to the podcast.

41
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
So, Ted, hello.

42
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Nice to meet you,

43
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Ted Rotol, up there in St Charles Illinois,

44
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And Tony said hi too. by the way,

45
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Today on the Scout Master Podcast, we're going to talk with Peter LaRue.

46
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Peter is a group scout leader in South Africa and he also attended the International Jamboree this summer

47
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And he is also the author of a blog about pioneering called Ropes and Polls.

48
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Peter and I had a wide ranging discussion about a lot of interesting things. you know kind of comparing and contrasting what it's like to be a scout in South Africa

49
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:19,000
and what it's like to be a scout here, and the spoiler alert on that one is pretty much the same.

50
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,000
I really enjoy talking with international scouts because it just cements that idea of worldwide movement

51
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
and the applicability of all of these scouting principles wherever they happen to land,

52
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
And I'm anxious to get to the interview. So let's get started, shall we

53
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Music

54
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Today on the Scout Master Podcast. I'm very happy to welcome Peter LaRue from South Africa.

55
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Peter LaRue, that might be a familiar name to some of you. he's the author of the Ropes and Polls blog,

56
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:15,000
A blog that principally concentrates on pioneering, but we get to see some interesting things about international scouting

57
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
and scouting in South Africa. How are you today, Peter?

58
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
I'm good. thanks, Clark. I've had a great day so far.

59
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
It's Saturday here. it's Saturday there, am I correct?

60
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Yeah, it's Saturday evening

61
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And springtime there. yes,

62
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Yeah it is. We had the first rains of the season this week,

63
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
So it's springtime, but you had a bribe, a barbecue, this afternoon,

64
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So it's been a pretty warm day.

65
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
So tell me a little bit about the area that you live in, because I'm still amazed by this, Peter.

66
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
I'm still amazed that I can get on the computer and speak with somebody in South Africa in real time.

67
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
basically halfway across the world

68
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,000
There's a town called well, a city called Bononi, which is about 30 kilometers away from Johannesburg,

69
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and it's really a continuous urban area.

70
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:10,000
There's about 9 million people that live in the urban area around Johannesburg.

71
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:16,000
At the same time, I'm also right on the edge of that area,

72
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So if I'm half an hour driving in one direction, I'm in the middle of the city

73
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and in the other direction I can be in a nature reserve,

74
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
So I'm very lucky. in terms of scouting,

75
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
It's very easy. Our local campsite is a 10-minute drive away

76
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and then half an hour down the road I can be out in the real backcountry felt areas.

77
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,000
What does that look like?

78
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Most of the time when we see South Africa on the television or something like that,

79
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
it's lions and grasslands and things like that.

80
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Is that what we're thinking of?

81
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,000
Well, where I live, it's mostly open, flat country.

82
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
In fact, where my house is, there are a lot of wetlands around,

83
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So there are a few lakes and marshes.

84
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
There's not a lot of trees here,

85
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
It's mostly crossland.

86
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Like I said, the area where I live is 9 million people living here.

87
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,000
In terms of wildlife, there's not a lot of big animals around still,

88
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
except in place names.

89
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
There's an elephant fountain and a lion river and that sort of thing.

90
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:31,000
But in terms of big wildlife, there are some antelope around, things

91
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
like porcupines and otters, owls, eagles,

92
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:42,000
But on the whole, as much as my scouts like to joke with people,

93
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
the on-lines walking down the street,

94
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Well, except occasionally, maybe, Who knows?

95
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Yes, well, there was a hyena that came into the suburbs around here a few years back,

96
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:00,000
But of course, as much problem as it would if a cougar came into the suburbs

97
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
in Florida. I suppose

98
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
What's your role in scouting there in South Africa?

99
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
I am a troop scouter,

100
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Not only in Grayspear. we have two troops in my scout group.

101
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
We have a boy troop and a girl troop.

102
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Scouting has been open to girls to join what was formerly the boy scout movement

103
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
for about the last 15 years.

104
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
So we have two troops, two separate troops

105
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
At the moment. we do a lot of activities together

106
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:36,000
But I have about 24 boys in four patrols and we meet on a Friday night

107
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
And you do basically the type of thing that scouts do everywhere.

108
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Yes, I mean it seems, having spoken to a few scouts from actually from Pennsylvania,

109
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
that our programs are very similar.

110
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Basic terminology might be different

111
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
And the things, the emphasis on things, is a little different.

112
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:58,000
You know, when my guys learn about survival situations,

113
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
they don't learn much about, say, surviving in the snow,

114
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
And we deal with local conditions.

115
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
But on the whole our program would be recognizable to your scouts.

116
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
One event that you featured on the blog that is just fascinating to me

117
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
and that is the Contiki camp that you guys do annually.

118
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
What is that all about?

119
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
I think it was in the early 50s,

120
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
For Hayadol, who's a Norwegian anthropologist,

121
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:35,000
had this idea that he could recreate the journeys that the Polynesian islanders used

122
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
to travel from island to island,

123
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
And he had this idea of building a raft out of Balsawood

124
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,000
and sailing at 4,000 miles across the South Pacific,

125
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:52,000
And in fact, a lot of the people who joined him on their journey were former scouts.

126
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:59,000
And in the mid 1960s, there were a few scouts from South Africa who liked the idea

127
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:06,000
and they built a raft and they spent 24 hours living on this raft

128
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
on one of the bigger dams in South Africa.

129
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And then, after that, in the early 1980s,

130
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
they picked it up as a pioneering competition in our province,

131
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:23,000
And every year, between 70 and 100 rafts take part in the competition,

132
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:30,000
which is part of it is about living on the water on a raft that you've built for 24 hours.

133
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
But then, on top of that, there's always a theme,

134
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
so you have to decorate your raft to match the theme.

135
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:40,000
So, for example, in 2011,, the theme was Contiki goes to Rome.

136
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So you had rafts decorated like chariots, like Roman temples.

137
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:49,000
You had scouts dressed up in togas and with wreaths on their heads

138
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And that's what they do.

139
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:57,000
It is the biggest annual event in South African scouting

140
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
And it's a lot of fun.

141
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:04,000
A lot of troops who take part spend the better part of the year preparing for it,

142
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And people like I say: when you're spending 24 hours living on your raft,

143
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:15,000
you concentrate on time your square elections properly.

144
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
It is absolutely fascinating.

145
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
It's something that I would just love to try.

146
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And how many rafts did you say participant?

147
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,000
I think the most we've had is around 120..

148
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
This last year, I think there were 70 teams.

149
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Wow, I mean, that's just huge.

150
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
And so everybody is floating on this impoundment on this lake

151
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And that is I'll tell you. I'll have a link to a couple of Peter's posts

152
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
on his blog about the Contiki thing.

153
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
You got to see it.

154
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
It is just amazing

155
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
If I could get my fellows to South Africa with a raft.

156
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Well, hey, that's not a bad idea.

157
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
That is just a really cool idea.

158
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
We actually have a BSA troop at the American School in Johannesburg

159
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
and they participate this year.

160
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
So there is a small BSA involvement in Contiki.

161
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
I think this year was the first time there,

162
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
But, speaking to the troop scout, they'll need to be back next year.

163
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
We talked a little bit about the fact that you have scout troops.

164
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
You have cub groups as well.

165
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Tell me about the group concept.

166
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
You said that you're a group scouter

167
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And that's unfamiliar to us here.

168
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
How does that work?

169
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:42,000
The structure here is that we have scouting groups in three age groups.

170
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
We have cub scouts from 7 to 11..

171
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
We have scouts from 11 to 18..

172
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
And then we have rover scouts,

173
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:54,000
which is roughly equivalent to venture scouts or velocity teams

174
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:01,000
from 18 up to 30-ish.

175
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:06,000
So those branches are generally in South Africa

176
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
would all be members of a group.

177
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
So a group consists of a cub pack, a scout troop

178
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and optionally a rover group.

179
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
So I was a cub at Nantes Benoni

180
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Cub pack and when I left cubs

181
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:24,000
I naturally went on to the scout troop at Nantes Benoni

182
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
And that is generally how scouting works in South Africa.

183
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Every cub pack is attached to a scout troop,

184
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
Every scout troop is attached to a cub pack

185
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:44,000
And that group committee forms sort of carries out

186
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,000
a lot of the functions of a sponsoring body in other movements.

187
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:52,000
So if you're sponsored by a church or a school or whatever,

188
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
then those functions are carried out by the committee

189
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
in terms of maintenance and administration,

190
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
looking after the property and that sort of thing.

191
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
I'm fascinated by the group concept

192
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
because I think there's many things that recommend it

193
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
And I think it's something that we should study here

194
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and see if there's some aspects of it we should adopt.

195
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
You've described a rather smooth transition into a scout troop

196
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and that's something that we worry over quite a bit here in the States.

197
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Do you see that

198
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Roughly what percentage of boys and girls from a cub pack

199
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
end up in scout troops in South Africa?

200
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
I would imagine

201
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
I don't know of hand, but on the whole

202
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:40,000
I would say it's probably between 80 and 90 percent.

203
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
that would come through smoothly.

204
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,000
That's an enviable percentage for us.

205
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
You compared rovers to explorers or varsity scouts

206
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
or venture scouts.

207
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
All of our youth programs pretty much stop at age 21..

208
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,000
But rovers can be rovers into their 30s. you say

209
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
I think globally the idea with rovers

210
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,000
I mean in other countries they said the limit is around 25..

211
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Historically here there hasn't been a limit.

212
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
if you invest as a rover

213
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
as long as you are invested as a rover before you turn 35,

214
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
you can stay a rover forever.

215
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
That's historically been what's happened here.

216
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
But in terms of recruiting new rovers

217
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
it becomes a different proposition

218
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:26,000
to try and recruit a new rover into the crew

219
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
when the rest of the crew is 50 or 60 or 70,

220
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
because an 18-year-old might not feel he has anything in common.

221
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So that's why the age limit's being bought in.

222
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:39,000
The idea really is that rovering is covering that part of your life

223
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,000
when you're leaving home, setting up your career,

224
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
getting married, starting a family,

225
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
and it's to help you apply scouting principles to your life

226
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
through that period.

227
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
And specifically for people who feel they don't have

228
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
the inclination or the time to be a troop scout or a pack scout,

229
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
So they don't necessarily want to be involved

230
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
with running a troop,

231
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
but they do want to stay involved in the movement.

232
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
That's who it serves.

233
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:10,000
I was in Switzerland at Kandersteg this year,

234
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Met a lot of rovers

235
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Again, something that I think would be a very interesting aspect

236
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
of scouting that's pretty much unknown here.

237
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
People do tend to stay involved,

238
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
but you kind of cross the Rubicon into this adult leadership world.

239
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
It's just a different way of looking at things

240
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:30,000
and a different way of experiencing the program.

241
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
You attended the

242
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Which international jamboree? what number was it? this year

243
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
It was the 22nd,

244
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
The 22nd international jamboree, and that was in Sweden. yes,

245
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,000
That's right.

246
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
And so tell me a little bit about that experience.

247
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,000
For me it was a fantastic experience.

248
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
We,

249
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Our contingent- all in all from South Africa

250
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
was 135 people,

251
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
of which 105 were scouts,

252
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
12 were unit leaders

253
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
who were traveling with the three scouts-

254
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
The three scout troops

255
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
And the remainder were ISD

256
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
International Service Team and then contingent staff-

257
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:11,000
And we joined 40,100 other scouts

258
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
in Kristianstatt, which is in the south of Sweden,

259
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
for the jamboree.

260
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
The jamboree itself was 12 days long.

261
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
Prior to the jamboree

262
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
we traveled in Germany for a week.

263
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
We were hosted by the DPSG,

264
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
who's the Catholic Scout Association in Germany,

265
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
And we then spent three days

266
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
on home hospitality

267
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
living with scout troops in Denmark.

268
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
The four patrols in my troops stayed

269
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
in three different towns for that,

270
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
but that was also quite an experience to see.

271
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
So we got to experience a bit of German scouting

272
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and Danish scouting

273
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and then got to meet everybody else at the jamboree.

274
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
What do you take away from an experience like that?

275
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,000
It must be just an incredible opportunity

276
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
to get a real interesting look

277
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
at scouting throughout the world.

278
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:04,000
One of the things that was very interesting

279
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
for a lot of us is we came away from it

280
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
realizing how similar our programs are.

281
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
I got to speaking.

282
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
I had long conversations with.

283
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
we had neighbors from Denmark

284
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
and a few people from Belgium.

285
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
I spoke to some Australians

286
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and some American scouts

287
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
And to realize how,

288
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
although historically and culturally

289
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
and in terms of background

290
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
were quite different,

291
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
that the scouting principles are very similar

292
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
And, more important than that,

293
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
that the ideals of the promise and law

294
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
are so similar around the world

295
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
And that, although at times

296
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
there was a language gap

297
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
and there was difficulty in communicating,

298
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
we were all there with the same intention

299
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
And that was something

300
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
that a lot of my scouts remarked on as well.

301
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
And I think, if you look back to

302
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
when Baden Powell had the idea

303
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
of a chapter, this idea of

304
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
people learning

305
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
to cooperate

306
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
internationally was a big part

307
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
of why he did it

308
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
And that came through very strongly

309
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
for my scouts and for me.

310
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
It would be difficult to hold a stereotype

311
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
or dislike people who you've

312
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
spent a couple of weeks camping with.

313
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
I mean, that was really

314
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
his intention, I think.

315
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
And I think that was

316
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
the first time across for us,

317
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
whereas I think there were 156 countries

318
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
represented at the Jamboree.

319
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
So for most of my scouts

320
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
and I'm sure a lot of other people,

321
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
it was the first time they made someone

322
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
from Bhutan

323
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
or from South Korea

324
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
or from Brazil

325
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
or Italy.

326
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
So it was a really good opportunity

327
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
to get to know those people better.

328
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
We found that when we traveled to

329
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
the background

330
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
An immediate, shared way

331
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
of looking at the world

332
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
that kind of surpassed any differences that we had.

333
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
One of the great stories

334
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
from the Jamboree was

335
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
at the opening ceremony

336
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
there was an arena

337
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
which was sort of

338
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
a little valley

339
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
off to the one side of the campsite

340
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
and at the bottom of the valley was a stage

341
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
and we all sat on the slopes

342
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
of this valley facing the stage

343
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
and 40,000 people had to move

344
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
from the sub-camps in their campsites.

345
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and speaking to the police

346
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
officers off to it who were there

347
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
to provide safety, they said they were

348
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
so surprised that

349
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
40,000 people got to this

350
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
opening ceremony and stayed for the concert

351
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
and got back off to it. so there wasn't a single

352
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
incidence of violence

353
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
or a disagreement or anything like that reported

354
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and the police

355
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
were really surprised because they never dealt with that

356
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
in a public gathering-

357
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
all in Sweden There's always

358
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
a scuffle that breaks out somewhere-

359
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
but because all of these people,

360
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
with 40,000 of us, were there for the same reason

361
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
and we were all making friends

362
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
and trading badges

363
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
and being there. for the same reason,

364
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
it went very smoothly.

365
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Obviously, you described a trip

366
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
that took about a month

367
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
altogether. Yeah, three weeks,

368
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
21 days out of the country,

369
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
and you had to travel

370
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
a pretty respectable distance

371
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
to get there.

372
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
Yeah, I think our total

373
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
travel distance was

374
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
in the region of 22,000 km

375
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
there and back. The interesting thing

376
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
is, for some of my scouts

377
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
who had never traveled outside

378
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
the country before, it was their first time

379
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
flying on an aeroplane,

380
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
their first time flying

381
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
or their first time traveling on a ferry,

382
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
their first time

383
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
traveling in an underground train,

384
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
their first time on a tram, which we did in Berlin.

385
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
so it was really

386
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
a big

387
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
whole new world of experiences

388
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
for them. It was probably a bit costly

389
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
as well, wasn't it? In today's exchange rate,

390
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
$3,500,

391
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
purchasing parity wise,

392
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
it's a significant amount of money

393
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
in South Africa.

394
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
It was quite

395
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
an expensive trip.

396
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
That's the interesting thing. there were some great stories

397
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
from our contingent.

398
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
one of the boys in my troop

399
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
phoned into a local radio station

400
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
and won a general knowledge competition

401
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
and that paid

402
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
for half of his trip.

403
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
One of the boys sold hot dogs

404
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
outside a shopping centre

405
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
for two years to pay for his trip,

406
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and then

407
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
one of the girls in my troop was also

408
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
able to get sponsorship from local companies

409
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
and she went around from company to company

410
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
with a letter explaining

411
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
what Scouting did for the community

412
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
and what she was hoping to do in Sweden

413
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
and sponsorship for the entire troop.

414
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
the overall point being,

415
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
if you are a scout

416
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
or a scouter, you really must

417
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
find one of these opportunities

418
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
and take advantage of it.

419
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
It is a life changing experience

420
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
and it's not limited to

421
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
the world jamboree

422
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,000
or something like that. There are

423
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
many, many different opportunities

424
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
for international events

425
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
and travel for scouts,

426
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
For any scout pretty much going

427
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
to be the highlight of his time in scouting,

428
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
Absolutely. I mean

429
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
we've had in the past.

430
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
I've had scouts from our troop attend

431
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
the American national jamboree.

432
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
we've had scouts

433
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
from other countries-

434
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
I know definitely Germany-

435
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:21,000
attend Kontiki. here

436
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
we have another event which is

437
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:25,000
a 12 day

438
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
hike adventure in

439
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
the Cedarburg in South African.

440
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
a lot of international scouts

441
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
come to that as well

442
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
and it really is.

443
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
I know those scouts are still in touch

444
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
with people they meet when they were here

445
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
and it's a great chance

446
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
to see the world

447
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
and to see the world with people, to see the world

448
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
the same way that you do.

449
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
So my message for folks listening in:

450
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
it may seem difficult and expensive

451
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
and in many respects,

452
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
yes, it can be difficult and expensive.

453
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
it's a lot of arrangements to make,

454
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
a lot of things to do

455
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
and it's definitely something

456
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
that you should set your sights on,

457
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
if you can manage it at all,

458
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
and everybody can manage it.

459
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
it's just how you go about it.

460
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
what happens in

461
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
coming months in your scout group?

462
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
The next big event we have coming up,

463
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
is the next big international event,

464
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,000
which is the jamboree on the internet

465
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:21,000
and jamboree on the A,

466
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
which is the 14th and 15th of October.

467
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
we always have a camp

468
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
at our local campsite

469
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
and we also have some radio amateurs

470
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
who help us

471
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
get onto the radio waves and speak to scouts

472
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
from all over the world.

473
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
that's the next big thing we're doing.

474
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
and the other thing-

475
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
believe it or not, although it's in September,

476
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
we are looking forward

477
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
to contiki in march and preparing for that.

478
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
I have a lot of young scouts,

479
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
so there's a lot of training we have to do:

480
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
making sure everybody

481
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
is able to tie the mashings

482
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
and assemble the raft,

483
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
able to handle the raft on the water,

484
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
getting everybody through their

485
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
swimming proficiency badges

486
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
so it's safe for them to be on the water

487
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
and we are

488
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
probably planning

489
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
a hiking trip in December.

490
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
Tell me a little bit about the way

491
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
the youth leadership concept works

492
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
in South Africa. I imagine it's

493
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
somewhat similar. Do you have,

494
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
for instance, a senior patrol leader

495
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
in that type of thing?

496
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
In the South African Scouts Association

497
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
we called it a troop leader.

498
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
we then have

499
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
patrol leaders

500
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,000
and patrol leaders have an assistant patrol leader

501
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
and

502
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
the goal is

503
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
for those patrol leaders and the troop leader

504
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,000
to be driving the program

505
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
and running the troop meetings,

506
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
much as it is in the

507
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
BSA system.

508
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
at the moment I have

509
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
some new patrol leaders

510
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
because I had three of my scouts

511
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,000
age out and turn 18 in the last

512
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
two months.

513
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
so their former

514
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
